## 🛠️ Troubleshooting
### NVIDIA GPU Compatibility
If you're using an NVIDIA GPU, you might encounter compatibility issues with PyTorch versions that are installed as dependencies. This typically occurs because the installed torch/torchvision versions might not match your GPU's CUDA version.

Solution: Install PyTorch with one version lower than your current NVIDIA CUDA version.

Example: If you have CUDA 12.2, install PyTorch for CUDA 12.1

### DuckDuckGo search to Tavily API
It's preferable to install this version of duckduckgo_search==6.3.5, if the latest version lauches the following error : `duckduckgo_search.exceptions.RatelimitException: https://duckduckgo.com/ 202 Ratelimit` 

Thus run the following command to install duckduckgo : ``pip3 install -U duckduckgo_search==6.3.5``
Solution found : https://github.com/open-webui/open-webui/discussions/6624 

Due to persistent rate limit issues and the unreliability of DuckDuckGo for more robust search requirements, I transitioned to the [Tavily Search API] https://docs.tavily.com/documentation/api-reference/endpoint/search for a more scalable and consistent solution. To integrate Tavily with LangChain, follow this guide: : https://python.langchain.com/docs/integrations/tools/tavily_search/ 

### NLTK Tokenizers
The application requires specific NLTK tokenizers for text processing. I've included a function that automatically checks for and installs required tokenizers if they're missing.
If you encounter tokenizer-related errors, you can manually install them using:

    python -m ntlk.downloader name of the tokenizer or tagger 

Example :   `` python -m ntlk.downloader punk_tab ``


## 🛠️💡 Improvements

- [ ] **PDF Handling**: Currently, PDFs can only be added and rendered one at a time. Attempts to use st.tabs to render multiple PDFs result in only the first tab being displayed.
- [ ] **PDF Processing Speed**: Chunking PDFs with the `unstructured` module can be slow, especially when extracting tables and images.
- [ ] **Enhanced Rendering**: Consider integrating JavaScript components to improve PDF and content rendering.
- [ ] **Constrained Environments**: Optimize performance and functionality for environments with limited resources.
- [ ] **Docker Support**: Add Docker configurations to simplify deployment and reproducibility.
- [ ] **Agent Logs & Graph State**: The current method for updating the agent’s logs and graph state is primitive and could be enhanced for better tracking and visualization.
- [ ] **Modular Coding Agent Architecture**: To improve structure and performance, consider dividing the coding agent into three specialized sub-agents:
    - **Research Agent**: Explores the problem domain, analyzes folder and file structures, and gathers relevant context before coding begins.

    - **Coder Agent**: Plans and writes code based on the research agent’s insights, defines architecture, selects tools/libraries, and implements solutions.

    - **Reviewer Agent**: Reviews and executes code in a controlled, sandboxed environment, captures errors, evaluates outputs, and provides structured feedback iteratively.

Regarding improvements for adding Docker support, I found some interesting approaches for running the Ollama server and the frontend Streamlit application using Docker:

There are two main approaches:

- Separate Docker containers using Docker Compose: One container runs the Ollama server, and the other runs the frontend. Examples of this approach can be found here: 
    - [Streamlit + Ollama example](https://github.com/ricardobalk/streamlit-ollama)
    - [Docker + Flask RAG example](https://medium.com/@ion.stefanache0/docker-flask-rag-114c795de22c)
    - [Running multiple models in Docker with Ollama](https://generativeai.pub/run-multiple-models-in-a-docker-container-using-ollama-to-create-a-chatbot-that-talks-to-your-3d614e67560f)
    - [Build a ChatBot with Langchain, Ollama & Deploy on Docker](https://abvijaykumar.medium.com/ollama-build-a-chatbot-with-langchain-ollama-deploy-on-docker-5dfcfd140363)

    Other resources in relation to such approach:
    - https://stackoverflow.com/questions/78232178/ollama-in-docker-pulls-models-via-interactive-shell-but-not-via-run-command-in-t 
    - https://www.reddit.com/r/docker/comments/1htexwr/how_to_deploy_an_application_using_ollama/ 
- Single Docker instance running both Ollama server and frontend: In this approach, both the server and the frontend run within a single Docker container. An example of this can be found here: 
    - [Serving LLaMA 3 locally with Streamlit](https://blog.devops.dev/serving-llama-3-locally-with-streamlit-dc23c4fa133f)

